## Defects requiring clarification (7 October 2026)

1. LegacyMigration says preserve the old cloud folder, but normalizeWorkspaceProject enforces cloudRoot/id and rejects another folder (projectWorkspace.js lines 38-39). An old id lacking tmp with folder pages/ZenRayAssets/tmp-id cannot be injected into V2. Retain the legacy cloud and catalogue separately, link legacyProjectIds, and let V2 compute its canonical destination. (7 October 2026)
2. LegacyMigration names the start action without an exact payload. Client.mjs target becomes projectId, whereas service start reads payload.id and payload.project. A valid call must include both explicit id and project definition: Client.mjs start <id> '{"id":"<id>","project":{"id":"<id>","name":"<existing name>","sources":[...]}}'. Missing id fails, and using register directly risks replacing source lists instead of start merging them. (7 October 2026)
3. Client.mjs intentionally projects every result.project to id/sync/jobs, so status and inventory cannot themselves display fileIds, SHA256 or backups (Client.mjs final console.log). Direct readers must inspect Project-<id>.json inventory.localFiles/backups using the context state path, or a full BridgeClient read. V2 long history documents this path, but the migration should explicitly name it at the verification step. (7 October 2026)
4. zenray-collect retains active wording that every sent project bears a date in its identifier and that loose items create a daily tmp-date-divers project. This conflicts with preserving an existing stable project identity. Add a dated override: reuse existing mapped ID; dates are metadata; only a genuinely new project is named once. (7 October 2026)

## Verdict (7 October 2026)

- The destructive legacy purge is no longer recommended. Source scoping, keep, existing-source merge and archive retry match real V2 methods. The four documentation gaps above remain until their updated instructions are re-read. Runtime recovery remains unverified by this read-only test. (7 October 2026)

